  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏚️ Haunted House Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for haunted houses and Halloween attractions, from Blackwood Hall to the Not-So-Haunted House."
    },
    "intro": "A haunted house name sets the mood before anyone walks in: a grand old hall promises a ghost story, a pun promises fun for the little ones, and a cellar with a number promises screams. This **haunted house name generator** makes names for Halloween attractions, school fairs, ghost tours, escape rooms and games, with a tagline if you need one.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What makes a good haunted house name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>A real-sounding house:</strong> English country houses are called Hall, Grange, Manor, Court or Priory, so Blackwood Grange sounds like somewhere with a past. Borley Rectory in Essex, once called \"the most haunted house in England\", shows how much an ordinary house name can carry.</li><li><strong>An address:</strong> \"The House on Gallows Lane\" works because it sounds like the start of a ghost story.</li><li><strong>A promise:</strong> the name should tell people how scared to expect to be. Puns and cosy words (Cobweb Cottage) say family fun; a numbered cellar or motel says screams.</li><li><strong>A forgotten place:</strong> old mills, piers, theatres and schools make good settings because everyone can picture them empty.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming a Halloween attraction",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Match the name to the scare level,</strong> and put an age guide on the poster as well. Parents at a school fair will thank you.</li><li><strong>Add a tagline</strong> for posters and tickets: \"nobody leaves before midnight\" does a lot of work.</li><li><strong>Don't use a real address or a real tragedy.</strong> Make the place up, and leave real people's homes and real events out of it.</li><li><strong>Charging entry?</strong> Search the name first to check another local attraction isn't already using it.</li></ul><p>To fill the house, try the <a href=\"ghost-name-generator.html\">Ghost</a>, <a href=\"vampire-name-generator.html\">Vampire</a> and <a href=\"zombie-name-generator.html\">Zombie</a> name generators. For the party afterwards, the <a href=\"event-name-generator.html\">Event Name Generator</a> has party names.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What should I call a haunted house for children?",
        "answer": "Something that sounds friendly before anyone goes in: a pun or a cosy word, like Cobweb Cottage or the Not-So-Haunted House. Choose the family-friendly style."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you name a scary haunted attraction?",
        "answer": "Short and specific works best: a place and something wrong with it (The Rot Cellar), or a building with a number (Motel 31). Add a tagline to finish the job."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I name my haunted house after a real place?",
        "answer": "It's better not to. Make the place up, so you're not using a real person's home or a real tragedy, and check no local attraction already has the name."
      }
    ]
  }
